===== coverageTotalityLaw
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. A relation row carries a `coverageTotality` row only where the relation's own meaning makes its rung TOTAL over the snapshot inventory. A relation with no row declares that a subject may lawfully bear no fact, which is itself the record that nothing further constrains it.",
 "whyItIsNeeded": "`entry.coverage: complete` is a claim that the committed examined partition was examined exhaustively. For a rung that is total over the inventory that claim also decides a universal negative: over a COMPLETE `file@enumerated` Coverage, `exists file where path=P` is authoritative, so a complete result that retains no fact for an inventoried subject asserts that an inventoried file is ABSENT. On frozen v13 that graph closed - a `file@enumerated` scope over the inventoried subject `a.ts`, `coverage: complete`, `deficiency: null` and zero facts. Nothing derived the obligation, so nothing refused it.",
 "whichRelationsHaveOne": "`file@enumerated` only. A snapshot inventories exactly the files it contains, so every inventoried path has exactly one file fact and an omission is an error. `package@manifest-declared` and `vcs-change@vcs-reported` are NOT total and get no row: most paths declare no package and most paths were not changed, so their empty results are ordinary findings rather than omissions. Applying totality to them would manufacture obligations the relations do not carry.",
 "whatItDoesNotRequire": "Only subjects that are IN the snapshot inventory are owed a fact. A scope naming a path the snapshot does not contain is answered honestly by a complete result with no fact for it - the host examined the question and the file is genuinely absent. And the law is conditional on `coverage: complete`: an `unknown` result with its disclosed deficiency owes nothing, which is what keeps a partially examined inventory representable.",
 "enforcedAt": "identity-model close_run, over the RETAINED view's own facts and the retained snapshot inventory, alongside the other Coverage prerequisites. It reads no claimed value except the `complete` it is judging."
}
===== coveragePartitionLaw
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. The DISJOINTNESS half of identity-and-evidence section 3's `Coverage scopes partition the claimed universe without overlaps or omissions`. The OMISSION half is coverageTotalityLaw above and is owed only where a relation carries a coverageTotality row. These two halves have different owners and different decidability, and neither implies the other.",
 "whyItIsNeeded": "The clause was stated and nothing derived or compared it. A retained Run could carry two subject-scopes of ONE view with the same snapshot, relation, rung and both universes whose subject sets intersect, and close. What that demonstrates is that an INVALID partition was ACCEPTED: the same subject was committed twice under one interpretation, so a claim read over that view - a count, a completeness claim or a universal negative - can be ambiguous or double-counted depending on how a consumer joins the scopes. It is NOT asserted that every consumer necessarily double-counts; what is established, and what this law fixes, is that the published partition property was not decided anywhere. That is the same defect class as the frozen-reference file@enumerated omission.",
 "partitionKey": [
  "snapshotId",
  "relation",
  "resolution",
  "sourceUniverse",
  "targetUniverse"
 ],
 "keyRule": "Two subject-scopes of the same view are in the same partition exactly when they agree on EVERY field of partitionKey. Within one partition their `subjects` arrays must be pairwise disjoint. Scopes in DIFFERENT partitions may name the same subject freely: a different relation, a different rung or a different universe is a different claim about that subject, not an overlap. This is the same tuple coverageTotalityLaw uses for `matchOn`, deliberately, so the key that decides which fact discharges a scope's obligation is the key that decides which scopes are comparable at all.",
 "snapshotIsListedDeliberately": "Run closure independently requires every subject-scope to carry the Run's own snapshotId, so within one admitted Run this field is constant and listing it changes no outcome today. It is listed for the same reason coverageTotalityLaw lists it: this law reads a property of a SHARED view, and a join that silently depends on an invariant enforced elsewhere is the implicit coupling both laws exist to remove.",
 "scope": "PER VIEW, over every subject-scope the view references - INCLUDING scopes that carry no Coverage entry. A scope with no Coverage bypasses the per-Coverage PRODUCER guard (admit_coverage_result_v3 is never called for it), which is exactly the case a single-Coverage producer cannot decide; it does still reach the retained-scope ladder guard (SUBJECT_SCOPE_RUNG_NOT_IN_RELATION_LADDER) later in close_run, so saying it reaches no other check at all would be false. Distinct views of one Run may reference the SAME scope, and that is not an overlap: a view is one producer's interpretation, and the partition is a property of that interpretation.",
 "whatRemain
===== snapshotJoinLaw
"Normative. Each row states what the enclosing fact snapshot must contain for this relation payload to be admissible. Rows are applied by identity-model close_run on every owning fact; a relation absent from this table declares that it names no snapshot path and no retained artifact, which is itself the record that nothing constrains it."
